From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dbuf: take the dnode handles back on Linux

Commit ed87d456 ("Skip dnode handles use when not needed") made a dbuf
point at its dnode directly on Linux and FreeBSD, because "Neither
FreeBSD nor Linux currently implement kmem_cache_set_move(), which means
dnode_move() is never called", and the three zrlock round trips per dbuf
life cycle were paying for nothing. Now that the Linux allocator can ask
a cache to relocate its objects, they pay for something again: without a
handle in between, a dnode cannot move at all, and dnode_move() returns
KMEM_CBRC_NO for every object it is offered.

This only flips the switch back for Linux. The code behind it is the one
that shipped here until 2024 and is still what illumos uses, and FreeBSD
keeps the simplified path since nothing asks it to move anything.
---
diff --git a/include/sys/dbuf.h b/include/sys/dbuf.h
--- a/include/sys/dbuf.h
+++ b/include/sys/dbuf.h
@@ -213,7 +213,7 @@
 	 * Handle to safely access the dnode we belong to (NULL when evicted)
 	 * if dnode_move() is used on the platform, or just dnode otherwise.
 	 */
-#if !defined(__linux__) && !defined(__FreeBSD__)
+#if !defined(__FreeBSD__)
 #define	USE_DNODE_HANDLE	1
 	struct dnode_handle *db_dnode_handle;
 #else
